{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1563841-the-bane-incarna-ischin-as-an-enemy-faction-in-werewolf","id":1563841,"title":"The Bane Incarna Ischin as an enemy faction in Werewolf","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Werewolf: The Apocalypse"],"posts":[{"date":"2026-09-04T17:23:34-07:00","id":1563841,"title":"The Bane Incarna Ischin as an enemy faction in Werewolf","content":"Ischin is an orphaned Bane Incarna that, as far as I can determine, is mentioned only once - in the original Storyteller's Guide to the Sabbat! The spirit is part of a large Sabbat adventure whose main purpose is to return Sam Haight back to the material plane so that metaplot chronicle can proceed throughout the World of Darkness sourcebooks.<br /> <br /> Ischin is a Bane Incarna who was orphaned and trapped into a spirit realm by the Garou long ago, and is trying to escape. When Sam Haight escaped at the end of Valkenburg Foundation by suddenly disappearing into the Umbra by a special fetish (for reasons of metaplot), he randomly ended up at Ischin's spirit home/prison. Now the Incarna is using that fetish to engineer its own escape. That plan involves working with a Sabbat vampire building an interdimensional portal to explore the underworld (because he's on the Path of Death and the Soul), but Ischin is corrupting that vampire so instead it'll open up to Ischin's dimension and he'll escape. Sam Haight, now a prisoner of Ischin, sends dreams to the Sabbat PCs so they'll interfere with this plan, and somehow end up allowing him to escape instead of Ischin. This is absolutely essential because without it Sam Haight can't show up in Rage Across the Amazon next.<br /> <br /> In terms of Ischin itself, we learn some information about it.<br /> <br /> Its spirit servants can possess vampires.<br /> <br /> Its spirit servants seem to be able to possess/animate the dead (aka zombies), and who can then attempt to possess a vampire as one of its attacks.<br /> <br /> One of its spirit servants can coat itself in flesh using bits and pieces of bodies and able to walk. This might be a special kind of Materializtion Charm if we were to translate it into WtA terms. This Fleshy Body Creature has excellent combat stats (as its meant to take on an entire coterie of Sabbat vampire PCs).<br /> <br /> Other spirit servants can also materialize on the physical plane and can use powers like fifth level Dominate (which may just be the Possession charm), as well as Presence or anything else the ST wants.<br /> <br /> Ischin's physical manifestation (which happens only if the PCs fail to turn off the portal) includes powers equivalent to Auspex 10, Celerity 7, Dominate 9, Fortitude 8, Obfuscate 7, Presence 7, multiple paths of Thaumaturgy (Lure of Flames 6, Movement of Mind 6, Weather Control 6), and anything else the ST wants. So this is an extremely powerful entity in what I assume must be its Incarna Avatar form. It's not described as anything more than grotesque, but presumably crawls.<br /> <br /> And that's about it. Not much else. However, based on this limited information, we can tell Ischin still has a small spirit brood with it. While Ischin itself is imprisoned, it is possible for him to reach out to the material world, and his spirit brood can interact with the world as normal. The powers associated with this brood heavily involve possession, mind control, animation of the dead, and can interact with the dead or undead in general.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-09-04T17:28:22-07:00","id":1563842,"title":"","content":"I was not familiar with the term &quot;orphaned Incarna&quot;, but eventually found a reference to orphaned spirits in the first edition Umbra the Velvet Curtain. An orphaned spirit is basically a lesser spirit created by a higher ranked one which is subsequently destroyed. When that higher ranked spirit is destroyed (actually destroyed, not just sent to zero Essence and forced into Slumber), all the spirits it created must make a Gnosis check (difficulty 8) to survive. Those that do not, disappear forever. Those that manage to survive are &quot;orphaned&quot;.<br /> <br /> So if I am to take the description seriously, it means Ischin survived the destruction of some kind of Wyrm Celestine. It lost a lot of power though, and it was subsequently banished or imprisoned (perhaps either by a more powerful Gaian spirit, or perhaps some ancient Legendary Garou ritual).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-09-04T17:33:30-07:00","id":1563843,"title":"","content":"<br /> = = =<br /> Despite that this is a completely throwaway reference that has never been referenced again, I like doing archaeology like this and repurposing things like this into something more gameable.<br /> <br /> In general, I like the basic scenario a lot. We have a terrible eldritch entity trapped in an alien dimension prison trying to escape. It has spirit servants that are active in the Umbra and can materialize in the Realm, trying to help their master to escape. This is classic Lovecraftian style horror. It also has an affinity for the dead and undead. It is very hostile to life as we know it.<br /> <br /> I'll be taking elements from the Brian Lumley created Great Old One of Bugg-Shash and reskin it to Ischin. I'll link it to the Abyss. It has connections to Oblivion (Wraith) and Obtenebration (or whatever that Discipline is called now). Bugg-Shash is known for its preference for complete darkness, its ability to &quot;drown&quot; living people by covering them in viscuous black slime, and animating corpses by immersing them in that black slime. Such undead becomes its slaves which it completely controls. While this is not how Ischin's (or its spirit brood's) possession ability works, I think it's an evocative image and that's what I'm using. If it can control zombie slaves, murder the living and then control their corpses, then there's no reason why it cannot possess the dead bodies of vampires as well (it's just that already being dead, vampires can conceivably regain control of themselves from the slime and get rid of it).<br /> <br /> Bugg-Shash doesn't have its own cult because why would anybody want to worship and obey an entity that just wants to kill you. However, it is an entity some sorcerers like to summon in order to kill their enemies. Presumably though, it does have a &quot;cult&quot; of zombie slaves covered in black slime that does do whatever work Bugg-Shash wants of them.<br /> <br /> This is fairly easy to convert to Werewolf. Instead of just Ischin, I'm calling it Ischin the Black. Garou know some legends about it, and those legends explain it as a horror that slithered out of the Abyss. That is where the spirit brood of Ischin the Black is mostly found, though sometimes these Banes do escape and appear elsewhere. Many Garou believe Ischin was orphaned when Wolf saved Gaia by destroying the Great Darkness (see the Silver Fang legend in the first Werewolf Players Guide, pp. 82-83). As such, the Garou consider Ischin a most dangerous and malignant Bane Incarna. Silver Fangs of the Ivory Priesthood are the Garou who have done the most research on this Bane Incarna and know the most about him.<br /> <br /> Various Wyrm cults and sorcerers do summon the spirit brood of Ischin the Black to kill their rivals. This could be the Pretanic Order, Circle of Red, or any other sorcerous cult you invented from your home chronicles.<br /> <br /> In addition to this are the zombie slaves of Ischin the Black. These are generally foolish sorcerers who screwd up their rite of summoning, and were killed themselves. However, it also comprises the victims these spirits have murdered. This zombie &quot;cult&quot; has only one purpose - try to free Ischin from its prison. They obviously have never been successful, but are basically undead fetishes of these Banes that don't slumber. So they can be involved in all sorts of weird occult stuff. They mostly stay underground to avoid sunlight, but are sometimes active at night on the surface.<br /> <br /> Ischin the Black and its spirit brood might also have contact with the Abyssal Mystics of Clan Lasombra. There's no reason Lasombra vampires can't be equally stupid as mortal sorcerers, and they may not conceive that there are Abyssal spirits that they cannot control and can take them over. There also could be some crossover with vampires trying to understand death and the afterliufe, especially those on the Path of Death and the Soul, Path of Bones, or similar.<br /> <br /> If you assume a connection between the Abyss and Oblivion (which I do), it might mean that these spirits might manifest as terrifying Plasmic entities in the Tempest and prey on wraiths.<br /> <br /> It won't be a major faction, but it can be something that can pop up now and then. Maybe introduce the concept when the PC Garou pack fights some low powered Wyrm cult. Later encounter a spirit sent to kill someone (either mortal or Garou). Encounter similar spirits if they visit the Abyss. And later discover a cult of black slime zombies enacting obscene rites in the deepest underground lairs.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-09-04T18:59:57-07:00","id":1563847,"title":"","content":"What I think is interesting here:<br /> <br /> 1. It works better with an open minded Pack willing to talk to the undead.<br /> <br /> Not because this is strictly necessary, but that WtA already has plenty of ways to put players against undead wyrmspawn. Ischin shines as antagonist in a plot where the difference between its servants/victims and other sources of undeads is meaningful.<br /> <br /> 2. The concept can be expanded. Instead of Ischin as a faction, the most relevant take is Ischin as an example of Orphaned factions.<br /> <br /> Here I'd actually avoid making it known or able to manifest anywhere. Rather I'd lean on the idea of local phenomenon of disproportionate power. Ischin exist in a very limited realm. The sorcerers and vampires that contact it either do so in very specific places or under very specific circumstances. The Nation has almost no lore on this thing, the few past encounters having to be dealt with in the dark. It's even possible that several Septs faced Ischin at some point in the past without knowing of the other times it happened.<br /> <br /> Ischin is an example, and others exist. Things with too limited contact surface with reality at large to be noticed, but definitely a dangerous level of power when found, they're incomprehensible fragments of a forgotten past that have the actual potential to become the dominant future.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-09-05T20:20:45-07:00","id":1563863,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>monteparnas</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1563841-the-bane-incarna-ischin-as-an-enemy-faction-in-werewolf?p=1563847#post1563847\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Ischin shines as antagonist in a plot where the difference between its servants/victims and other sources of undeads is meaningful.</div> </div> </div> </div>Despite what I wrote, I hadn't considered the idea that Ischin could be an ongoing predator of the undead, and therefore could be a way for vampires and lupines to want to work together. That is an interesting angle.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>monteparnas</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1563841-the-bane-incarna-ischin-as-an-enemy-faction-in-werewolf?p=1563847#post1563847\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Rather I'd lean on the idea of local phenomenon of disproportionate power. Ischin exist in a very limited realm. The sorcerers and vampires that contact it either do so in very specific places or under very specific circumstances.</div> </div> </div> </div>Yes, I think that'd be a very good touch. Restrict the times and places where someone could contact Ischin or summon his spirit servitors to illustrate theat Ischin itself is restrained in his prison.<br /> <br /> I personally like adding flavor text requirements like this to all sorts of rituals, but usually that angers players because they want to do whatever they want at any time. However, since this would be NPC only, there wouldn't be any objection like that. Good idea.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>monteparnas</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1563841-the-bane-incarna-ischin-as-an-enemy-faction-in-werewolf?p=1563847#post1563847\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Ischin is an example, and others exist. Things with too limited contact surface with reality at large to be noticed, but definitely a dangerous level of power when found, they're incomprehensible fragments of a forgotten past that have the actual potential to become the dominant future.</div> </div> </div> </div>Also agree. I try to include a lot of Lovecraftiana (where appropriate) into Wyrm lore, mythology, legend, etc. So making certain things more difficulty to learn about is good sometimes. Of course, when that happens, you still need a way to instruct the PCs to learn that lore so that becomes an additional thing for the ST to figure out.<br /> <br /> I personally like some Werewolf plots to be more investigatory in nature, but I've found that often confuses players or sometimes even angers them because they just want to be combat monsters when they play Werewolf. Strangely, these same players don't seem to have problems in investigative scenarios when we play Call of Cthulhu. But even when I tell the players that certain scenarios will be like that in Werewolf, it rarely works out well for me.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-09-08T12:38:06-07:00","id":1563919,"title":"","content":"I just realized that an ST could make use of the possibility that Ischin might be one of the Talons of the Wyrms.<br /> <br /> Canonically, we have three candidates: Narlthus (from Rite of Passage), Koschei (from Rage Across Russia), and Thrassus/Exhibit 338 (from Book of the Weaver). I say &quot;candidates&quot; because at least in terms of Narlthus and Thrassus, it's just an option provided in the text and the ST could do something different with them. I believe Koschei is the only one that was directly stated to be since it was a plot point in Rage Across Russia. The other two candidates are unknown, and that assumes that the Talons are only five in number. In reality, there could be seven, nine, thirteen, or any number of Talons. Whatever the ST wants.<br /> <br /> Since Ischin is imprisoned, and we know the Talons are imprisoned, it opens up that possibility. Of course, Ischin doesn't need to be a Talon to be associated with the legends of the Talon. Maybe its creator was a Talon but destroyed, which is why it is an orphaned Incarna. It doesn't even need to be true that Ischin is a Talon or created by one. It could just be speculation by the Garou in your chronicle. One of many candidates who may or may not be a Talon. I personally like that kind of uncertainty. <br /> <br /> I don't think that was the intent when Ischin was casually introduced in the Storyteller's Guide to the Sabbat. If it was, surely the name would be used again in an actual Werewolf sourcebook. However, I think implying some kind of connection would increase the sense of menace around it. <br /> <br /> I think too many STs slavishly follow sourcebook canon, and I believe that hurts chronicles because it eliminates the sense of mystery. If players have read many sourcebooks, they already &quot;know&quot; so many things their PCs do not, that it sandbags the ST even when good players try to ignore that meta-knowledge. However, if an ST mentions nine possible candidates by name for the Talons for example, suddenly those well read players do not have any more knowledge than a brand new player. Does that mean there are more than five? Which of these nine are the five Talons? Are any of them Talons, or are there unknown threats out there? What happens when these septs say five, but others nine, and some use different numbers? By introducing the unknown, it creates mystery and fear, and I think that's a good for Werewolf chronicles.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}